WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.630
Spider-Man's homecoming to the Marvel Cinematic
Universe has been a huge success for both

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.140
Marvel Studios and Sony, but as amazing as
Spidey's new solo movie is, there were still

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.780
numerous scenes and ideas that were deleted
and rejected from the final film.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.789
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video, I'll be explaining some

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:21.989
fascinating scenes and concepts that never
made the final cut of Homecoming involving

00:00:21.989 --> 00:00:27.109
a whole range of characters such as Nick Fury,
Vulture, Aunt May, Tony Stark and Captain

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:31.179
America, and I'll be exploring the use of
Peter Parker's Spider Sense in the movie.

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:34.820
I'm also running a Spider-Man: Homecoming
giveaway at the moment for a couple of Funko

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.570
Pops, plus a pack of Homecoming merchandise.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.750
For a chance to win, make sure you subscribe,
hit the bell for notifications, and leave

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:46.440
me a comment with your thoughts about the
deleted scenes or any future Marvel movies.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.340
And quick warning: there are spoilers ahead
for Spider-Man: Homecoming, so take care if

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.109
you haven't seen the movie yet.

00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:57.129
In many ways, it seems totally logical that
Homecoming would continue to develop Tony

00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:00.250
Stark's mentor-style relationship with Spider-Man
from Civil War.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:04.930
But originally, director Jon Watts had somebody
quite different in mind to fill that role.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:09.690
Yes, as part of a visual presentation before
he landed the job of director, Watts created

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.550
images of former SHIELD director Nick Fury
as Peter Parker's mentor.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:17.230
When you think about it, it's hardly surprising
that initially Watts thought of featuring

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:21.860
Nick Fury in the movie especially given the
influence on the film of Brian Michael Bendis

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.740
and Mark Bagley's Ultimate Spider-Man comics,
in which Nick Fury plays a prominent part

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.380
and is even viewed as a sort of father figure
by Peter.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.570
It would have been intriguing to see just
which aspects of that comic-book interaction

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.290
between Fury and Spidey that Watts would have
brought across to Homecoming.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:41.020
And it would have been nice to see Samuel
L Jackson back as Fury given that we've not

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.950
seen him since 2015's Age of Ultron.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.820
Of course, Tony Stark's presence in the new
Spidey-verse provides the MCU with an easy

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.450
explanation for how such a young guy has such
an incredibly tricked-out suit, as Kevin Feige

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:54.710
himself has said.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:59.100
But, ultimately, the reason for Iron Man's
inclusion in Homecoming over Nick Fury seems

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.060
to be that Tony Stark's presence in Peter
Parker's world was one of the keys to the

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.640
deal that Marvel brokered with Sony to bring
Spider-Man into the MCU fold.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:11.250
After all, Iron Man equals big bucks at the
box office.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:16.310
By the way, Tom Holland's next solo movie
as Spidey will also feature another Marvel

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:20.800
character, but, according to the Hollywood
Reporter, it's unlikely to be Iron Man next

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:21.800
time.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.440
Maybe that means there'll be room for Nick
Fury to step in to the role of guiding Spidey

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.440
through his early years as a superhero, as
director Jon Watts originally envisaged.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.930
Or perhaps Captain America's role could be
expanded beyond those PSA cameos, and, actually,

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:39.070
that would be something Tom Holland would
be totally down with as, in a recent interview

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:44.240
with IMDB, when asked to choose another Avenger
as a mentor for Spidey, Holland immediately

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.550
picked Captain America, even though he thinks
Cap would be much stricter than Tony Stark!

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:53.630
And in the meantime, it looks like, instead
of mentoring Peter Parker, Nick Fury could

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.570
be appearing in Captain Marvel's first solo
movie.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:01.040
The extent or nature of Fury's role in the
Captain Marvel film hasn't been confirmed

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.560
yet, but it wouldn't surprise me if he took
on the part of a mentor for Carol Danvers

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.190
in a similar way that Tony Stark did with
Peter Parker in Spider-Man: Homecoming.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:15.290
I've gotta say I did enjoy Tony Stark's role
in the film and, for me, it didn't feel overwhelming,

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:19.060
which, given the marketing for the movie,
I had thought it might do.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.160
Still, marketing for the film definitely did
play up Iron Man's role in the movie with

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.420
several Iron Man and Tony Stark shots in the
trailer which didn't make it into the final

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.420
feature.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:32.080
For example, this shot of Spider-Man and Iron
Man flying was wanted specifically by the

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.020
movie's marketing team for use in the trailer.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:38.370
There was footage of the two together from
the Staten Island Ferry scene, but Jon Watts

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:42.460
felt the background was too plain, so, instead
they decided to put them together with a shot

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.440
of Queens in the background.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.890
And there's another scene with Tony Stark
and Peter Parker which was cut down quite

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.870
a bit from what we saw in the trailers.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:54.030
It takes place when Tony has his chat with
Peter when they arrive back in Queens after

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.800
the events of Civil War.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.790
Peter's excited that he's going to be a big
part of the Avengers now, but Tony's downplaying

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.880
it with a mix of his trademark dry humour
and one-liners.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.599
I suspect what happened here is that they
shot more lines and gags than were needed,

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:09.920
and maybe Robert Downey Junior and Tom Holland
even improvised some.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.569
"So to become an Avenger, are there like trials
or an interview?"

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:13.569
"Just don't do anything I would do."

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:15.200
"This does not mean you're an Avenger.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.880
Case you were wondering."

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:18.880
"Oh."

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.829
"I can keep the suit?"

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:21.829
"Course, doesn't fit me."

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:22.840
"All right kid, good luck out there!"

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:27.020
Captain America: Civil War introduced the
MCU to a very different Aunt May than we've

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.930
seen before on the big screen.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:33.770
Marisa Tomei's May is a much younger woman
who Tony Stark can't resist flirting with.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:38.150
But two months later, during the main timeline
of Spider-Man: Homecoming, when she and Peter

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.729
are at a Thai restaurant, May comes right
out and says she's not a fan of Tony Stark.

00:04:42.729 --> 00:04:46.860
And Marisa Tomei revealed in an interview
with Good Morning America that there's a deleted

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.800
scene of her and Tony which I think sounds
like it would clarify why she doesn't like

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:51.800
him.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.510
Tomei says she filmed a scene where she was
at a protest against Stark Industries and,

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:57.949
at one point, she and Tony Stark eyeballed
each other.

00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:02.550
Tomei didn't elaborate any further, so we
don't know for sure what the protest was about,

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.710
but I wonder if was anything to do with the
fallout from Civil War.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:09.500
Prior to Civil War, Captain America was a
war hero and there was probably still sympathy

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.930
towards him in some sectors of the public.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:16.229
Tony Stark, on the other hand, is a billionaire
former CEO, so there's no shortage of things

00:05:16.229 --> 00:05:19.849
he could have done to draw anger from the
public and it's possible some people blamed

00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:21.800
him for splitting up the Avengers.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:25.560
Then there are people like Michael Keaton's
Toomes who are angry at the rich and powerful

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.740
who he sees as rigging the system in their
favour at the expense of ordinary hard-working

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:30.819
guys like himself.

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:35.169
So, I'm curious whether this deleted scene
was going to expand even further on this theme

00:05:35.169 --> 00:05:37.150
which was already in the movie.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:40.610
And remember, in the film, Michelle says she
wants to do some protesting in Washington

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.180
before the Academic Decathlon.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.590
Well, we never got to see that either, so
maybe that's the scene Tomei is referring

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.590
to.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.150
I think it's a shame we didn't get May's protest
scene as it would have helped fill in a little

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:55.499
bit of missing story for her character and
why she doesn't seem to like Tony Stark.

00:05:55.499 --> 00:05:58.870
I did enjoy Tomei's portrayal of Aunt May,
but I think there could have been a little

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:00.729
more for her to do in Homecoming.

00:06:00.729 --> 00:06:05.249
In fact, Tomei has said that she and Jon Watts
worked on a lot of details about May's character

00:06:05.249 --> 00:06:09.120
which didn't make it into the film and there's
a trace of another deleted scene with her

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.120
in the trailers.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:13.949
You can see here in this very short shot that
May's walking into what could be a classroom

00:06:13.949 --> 00:06:17.139
or an office with a worried look on her face.

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:20.789
And if you look carefully behind May, there's
an actress who we didn't see in the rest of

00:06:20.789 --> 00:06:26.319
the movie and I think this could be Jona Xiao
who, in August 2016, was reported by Variety

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:28.379
as having been cast in Spider-Man: Homecoming.

00:06:28.379 --> 00:06:32.809
Xiao, who's known for work on the shows Halt
And Catch Fire and Being Mary Jane, wasn't

00:06:32.809 --> 00:06:37.189
credited in the final film and her agent has
confirmed that her scene was cut from the

00:06:37.189 --> 00:06:38.189
movie.

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:41.669
And according to an interview with Polygon,
Marisa Tomei pushed for Xiao to be cast as

00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:45.439
this character who, she says, "is part of
a revelation that happens in the film, at

00:06:45.439 --> 00:06:46.810
a very dramatic point."

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:51.289
All of this indicates a potentially very interesting
scene cut from the movie that hopefully we'll

00:06:51.289 --> 00:06:53.339
see on the Blu-ray release.

00:06:53.339 --> 00:06:57.689
We've already seen a couple of other main
love interests on the big screen for Spider-Man

00:06:57.689 --> 00:07:02.649
with Mary Jane in the Sam Raimi trilogy and
Gwen Stacy in the Andrew Garfield movies,

00:07:02.649 --> 00:07:07.189
so Homecoming, instead, introduced a new crush
for Peter Parker, Liz Allen.

00:07:07.189 --> 00:07:11.499
Although this film does build up their romantic
connection, it seems like the filmmakers thwarted

00:07:11.499 --> 00:07:12.889
any real romance.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:17.430
First, we had that call-back to Spider-Man's
upside-down kiss with MJ in the first Tobey

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:22.550
Maguire movie when, in this film, Spidey is
hanging upside down opposite Liz after saving

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.449
her from the plummeting elevator.

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:28.759
Spidey's suit lady Karen even says to him,
"This is your chance Peter, kiss her", but

00:07:28.759 --> 00:07:31.960
the moment is taken away when the webbing
holding Peter breaks.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:36.309
And actress Laura Harrier who plays Liz Allen
said, in an interview with ComicBook.com,

00:07:36.309 --> 00:07:40.509
that she filmed "a lot of different kissing
scenes [but] none of them are in the movie

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:41.509
now".

00:07:41.509 --> 00:07:45.030
And in fact, we did glimpse a kiss between
them at the Homecoming Dance in the trailers,

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:47.589
but this was cut from the final movie.

00:07:47.589 --> 00:07:51.149
And given that Liz is going to move away,
I'm guessing the filmmakers are closing down

00:07:51.149 --> 00:07:53.900
any future romance between the two in the
sequel.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:58.979
So, it'll be interesting to see if they take
the route of hooking Peter up with MJ.

00:07:58.979 --> 00:08:03.229
I don't know about you but I loved Captain
America's PSAs, which Homecoming director

00:08:03.229 --> 00:08:08.470
Jon Watts got the idea for from a long-running
US programme to get kids fit called the President's

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:09.470
Challenge.

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:13.690
Of course, we got to see Cap speak to America's
youth on subjects from keeping fit to getting

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:18.860
detention, and we even saw the start of a
'so, your body's going through changes' PSA,

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:24.149
and there was also that cheeky little fourth-wall-breaking
one about patience in the post-credits.

00:08:24.149 --> 00:08:28.819
But, according to Jon Watts, he filmed a load
more PSAs with Chris Evans as Cap that didn't

00:08:28.819 --> 00:08:33.419
make it into the final movie, including one
about brushing your teeth, and there should

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:36.479
be even more of those to come when the Blu-ray's
released!

00:08:36.479 --> 00:08:40.279
Speaking of Cap, his shield got a shout-out
in the scene where Happy Hogan is arranging

00:08:40.279 --> 00:08:42.899
the move of Avengers tech to their new facility.

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:47.350
The items Happy mentions in the final film
are Cap's shield, Tony's Hulkbuster armour,

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:52.339
and Thor's magic belt, but originally director
Jon Watts co-wrote a whole page of monologue

00:08:52.339 --> 00:08:56.660
for that scene listing out a big range of
items that Happy was sorting out the transport

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:57.660
for.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:02.089
Now, Watts is a fellow easter egg fan so loved
loading up his movie with as many as possible,

00:09:02.089 --> 00:09:06.810
but I think, in this case, he made the right
decision to keep the moving-day scene tight,

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:10.880
mentioning just three items, even though that
means we lost a bunch more delicious Marvel

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:11.880
easter eggs.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:15.630
And, going back to the trailers, there was
yet another scene that didn't make it into

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:16.630
the movie.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:20.540
The shot of Vulture swooping down through
a hotel atrium was a scene that Jon Watts

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.560
put together specifically for a teaser at
Comic-Con.

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.750
It was never actually meant to be in the movie
and was only created because, at the time,

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:30.440
Watts didn't have enough material for the
sizzle reel that Marvel needed for the annual

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:31.830
San Diego event.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:35.650
The scene was filmed at the Marriott hotel
in Atlanta and although none of that shot

00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:39.980
made it into the final film, a similar take
of Vulture swooping down towards the camera

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:44.490
was used for the villainous character's entrance
in the movie, but this time with a completely

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:47.910
different backdrop; his crew's workshop.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:51.839
In the comics and on screen, the intuitive
Spider Sense that Peter Parker acquired after

00:09:51.839 --> 00:09:55.890
being bitten by a radioactive spider has been
an essential part of his story.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:59.639
And I feel like we do see his Spider Sense
on display at some points during his fight

00:09:59.639 --> 00:10:03.660
scene in Captain America: Civil War, though,
at other times in that film, he doesn't seem

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:08.410
to have it, despite the fact that Peter specifically
tells Tony Stark that the goggles on his home-made

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:11.800
suit help him focus since his senses have
been dialled up to 11.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:16.930
But in Homecoming, apart from a few moments
like where Peter web grenades a baddie without

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:21.700
looking at him, the filmmakers made a specific
creative decision to dial back Peter's Spider-Sense

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:26.589
for his first MCU solo movie, their reason
being that it's been seen so much already

00:10:26.589 --> 00:10:28.420
in Spidey's previous films.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:32.880
However, this doesn't mean that Peter's Spider
Sense is being abandoned altogether in the

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:33.880
MCU.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:37.509
Indeed, Kevin Feige has explained that their
intention is to make his Spider Sense more

00:10:37.509 --> 00:10:42.779
internal to Peter and less dramatic than it's
appeared in previous cinematic portrayals.

00:10:42.779 --> 00:10:46.810
All of which makes me think the fact that
Spidey sometimes appears to have his Spider

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:50.930
Sense and other times doesn't may be to do
with the fact that what we're seeing in these

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:55.720
movies now is a fresh take on Spidey's early
days as a superhero and they want to show

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:59.920
that he's still getting to grips with his
powers and doesn't have full control of them.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:04.279
I do hope that we see Peter's Spider Sense
progress in upcoming films as it's one of

00:11:04.279 --> 00:11:09.310
Spidey's greatest strengths and it's also
just one of the things I love about his character.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:13.400
By introducing Spider-Man in Civil War and
not going back in time with a prequel solo

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:17.720
movie, it meant that we didn't get Peter's
origin story, though frankly, I don't know

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:22.430
about you, but I really didn't need to see
him get bitten by a spider for the third time

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:26.829
so I'm kinda glad they skipped showing that
and, instead, gave it just a passing mention!

00:11:26.829 --> 00:11:31.370
And I also liked the filmmakers' new spin
on Peter Parker's work at the Daily Bugle,

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:35.819
which they updated by showing Peter's attachment
to videoing everything on his smartphone.

00:11:35.819 --> 00:11:40.750
But no full origin story also meant there
was no Uncle Ben to teach Peter that "with

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:42.819
great power comes great responsibility."

00:11:42.819 --> 00:11:46.779
It's a shame we didn't get that classic line,
which I love, but there was just the slightest

00:11:46.779 --> 00:11:51.170
allusion to a recent tragedy when Peter says
to Ned that he can't tell Aunt May about his

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.000
secret webcrawling activities.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:58.000
But really, overall, I thought it was better
that Uncle Ben wasn't dragged out again as

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:02.160
we've seen two recent iterations of him on
the big screen and, at this point, a third

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.350
Uncle Ben would have probably just felt repetitive.

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:10.180
I would like to see Uncle Ben's backstory
teased a little further in subtle ways in

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:15.279
the next film, however, there are fan theories
out there suggesting Uncle Ben is actually

00:12:15.279 --> 00:12:20.680
still alive in the MCU and, in a surprise
twist, could appear in a future movie.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:24.850
Now, what do you think about Spider-Man: Homecoming's
deleted scenes?

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:28.889
And how do you think Homecoming and Tom Holland's
Spider-Man compare with the Tobey Maguire

00:12:28.889 --> 00:12:31.459
and Andrew Garfield Spider-Man movies?

00:12:31.459 --> 00:12:34.199
I'd love to hear your thoughts in the comments
below.

